  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $56{\\text{\u00a0m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square decreasing when the sides are $8\\text{\u00a0m}$ long?",
  "worked_solution": "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14095797/assets/1cb323f4c38bb4fc13ffc81f47544424872480b42f033032da47c04d097e8696.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-56\\text{\u00a0m}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-56 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{28}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $8\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=8.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{28}{8}=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
